4":"rod","H6524":"to sprout","H2087":"arrogance","H2555":"violence","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H7562":"wickedness","H1995":"crowd","H1991":"wealth","H5089":"eminency","H5060":"to touch","H7069":"to buy","H408":"not","H8055":"to rejoice","H4376":"to sell","H56":"to mourn","H2740":"burning anger","H4465":"sale","H7725":"return","H5750":"still","H2416":"alive","H2377":"vision","H376":"man","H5771":"crime","H2388":"strengthen","H8628":"to blow","H8619":"trumpet","H3559":"prepare","H369":"nothing","H1980":"went","H4421":"battle","H2719":"sword","H2351":"outside","H1698":"pestilence","H7458":"famine","H1004":"place","H834":"which","H7704":"Sirion","H4191":"to die","H5892":"excitement","H398":"to eat","H6403":"to escape","H6412":"survivor","H3123":"dove","H1516":"Valley","H1993":"to roar","H3027":"hand","H7503":"to slacken","H1290":"knee","H3212":"to walk","H4325":"water","H2296":"to gird","H8242":"sackcloth","H3680":"to cover","H6427":"shuddering","H6440":"before","H955":"shame","H7218":"head","H7144":"bald spot","H3701":"money","H7993":"to throw","H2091":"gold","H5079":"impurity","H3201":"be able","H5337":"to rescue","H5678":"fury","H5315":"soul","H7646":"to satisfy","H4578":"belly","H4390":"to fill","H4383":"stumbling","H6643":"beauty","H5716":"ornament","H1347":"pride","H7760":"make","H6754":"image","H8251":"abomination","H6213":"do","H3651":"right","H2114":"be a stranger","H957":"plunder","H7563":"wicked","H7998":"spoil","H2490":"to bore","H5437":"turn","H6845":"to treasure","H6530":"violent","H7569":"chain","H4941":"judgement","H1818":"blood","H1471":"nation","H3423":"possess","H7673":"to cease","H5794":"strong","H6942":"consecate","H7089":"anguish","H1245":"to seek","H7965":"peace","H1943":"misfortune","H8052":"tidings","H5030":"prophet","H8451":"instruction","H6":"to perish","H3548":"priest","H6098":"counsel","H2205":"elder","H4428":"king","H5387":"leader","H3847":"to clothe","H8077":"devastation","H5971":"people","H926":"to dismay"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Israel, or Judea, in all its parts. (Worthington) — In five or six years’ time all shall be destroyed."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Set, as accusers, to increase thy confusion."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","One. A second shall not be necessary, Nahum i. 9. (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Awaked. Justice seems to have slept. (Haydock) See ver. 10., and Jeremias i. 12."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Destruction, (contritio.) St. Jerome reads “contraction,” as also [in] ver. 10. Protestants, “the morning.” (Haydock) — Chaldean, “the reign.” (Calmet) — Hebrew tsephira, is variously rendered. (Haydock) — Joy. Hebrew, “echo or cry” of people in the vintage."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Budded; figurative expressions, denoting a speedy vengeance. (Calmet) — Chaldean, “the reign is open, the ruler has flourished, the wicked hath appeared.” In this reign of Nabuchodonosor ye shall be chastised. (Haydock) — Septuagint or rather Theodotion, from whom many things have bee supplied according to St. Jerome, (Calmet) have, “The deceit (or thick foliage; ploke) has gone forth, the rod,” &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Iniquity. What other fruit could be expected from wickedness? It draws on chastisement. — Noise, or grandees. None will repent, and therefore none shall escape, from the highest to the lowest."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Sold, as in the year of jubilee, Leviticus xxv. 25. (Calmet) — Now there shall be no recovery. (Worthington) — They shall be driven from the land. — Although. By supplying the former negation, (Calmet) we may render “Nor,” &c., (Haydock) which seems better. (Calmet) — It may also mean, “those who are now alive” shall witness these things. (Haydock) Back. It is not a mere threat."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Ready. He describes the vain attempts of the Jews to make resistance."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Doves, not daring to vent their complaints. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Water; urine, (St. Jerome) sweat, &c., the usual effects of fear."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Heads. They cut their hair in times of penance and of mourning."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Iniquity. Riches have fostered their passions: they will not now procure food or liberty, Sophonias i. 18. (Calmet) — The riches of the idols invited the enemy to plunder. (Worthington)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","And. Protestants, “As for the beauty of his ornament, he set it in majesty: but they made the,” &c. The sacred ornaments of the temple, appointed by God, (Haydock) have been converted into idols, and shall be carried to Babylon, ver. 21. (Calmet)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Secret: the inward sanctuary, the holy of holies. (Challoner) — God would guard it no longer. (Calmet)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Shutting up. Hebrew, “chain,” for imprisonment and captivity. (Challoner) — Pronounce sentence upon all."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Sanctuary; the temple of God, or of idols, chap. vi. 3."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Distress. When the enemy is before the city, they will see their error; but it will be too late. They shall not obtain peace, Jeremias xxxviii. 1."],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Ancients. Priests shall afford no consolation, nor counsellors any advice. They shall be all confounded. (Calmet) — During the captivity a few only were found to give instruction, Malachias ii. (Worthington)"],["challoner",22,null,"challoner","Secret place, etc.... Viz., the inward sanctuary, the holy of holies."],["challoner",23,null,"challoner","Make a shutting up.... In Hebrew, a chain, viz., for imprisonment and captivity."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[15,16,0,"Commentary on the Gospel of Luke",null,0,444,"… \"They that are in the plain shall die by the sword: and them that are in the city famine and pestilence shall consume. And those of them that are saved shall be delivered, and shall be upon the mountains as meditative doves. For Israel did not perish from the very roots, nor, so to speak, stock and branch: but a remnant was delivered, of which the foremost and the first-fruits were the blessed disciples, of whom it is that he says, that they were upon the mountains as meditative doves. …"]],"fau":[["cyril-alexandria","Cyril of Alexandria",1,"father",null,1,444]],"ftr":[["R. Payne Smith (Oxford, 1859); transcribed and partly modernized by Roger Pearse"]]}